WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Think
sharks are scary?

00:00:00.065 --> 00:00:02.935 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:07.106 line:0%
Well, this monster fish can
eat a shark in a single gulp!

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:12.278 line:0%
MAN: Oh! Oh! Oh, my God!

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:15.748 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Weighing as
much as a grand piano,

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:19.618 line:0%
this territorial giant makes
headlines when it's hungry!

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.353 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:24.924 line:0%
Amateur video show goliath
grouper aggressively eating

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.627 line:0%
anything and everything
floating on the surface.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:31.931 line:0%
And craziest of all,
attacking scuba divers.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:35.401 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.770 line:0%
Once fished to near extinction,

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.807 line:0%
these protected ocean giants are
reclaiming their home turf.

00:00:41.907 --> 00:00:43.743 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:48.248 line:0%
So, I'm in Florida to catch a
super-sized goliath grouper

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:51.951 line:0%
and see if it really is the
monster it's made out to be!

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:54.420 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.322 line:0%
Oh, my God, it's huge!

00:00:56.422 --> 00:01:10.569 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:13.606 line:0%
Known internationally as a
fishing and dive capital

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.010 line:0%
of the world, people here
in Florida love to play in

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.011 line:0%
and around the water.

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:22.615 line:0%
But some people think there's a
potential monster-sized problem

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:25.685 line:0%
lurking beneath the surface
and it's not a shark!

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:32.391 line:0%
By the mid-1990s, goliath
grouper were so overfished,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:36.095 line:0%
they became listed as
critically endangered.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:38.031 line:0%
So, under current U.S. law,

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:41.334 line:0%
it's illegal to handle or
remove one from the water.

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:46.873 line:0%
So, to see how these
giants really behave,

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.075 line:0%
I'm gonna need to
call in the experts.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.980 line:0%
I'm out with Chris Malinowski
and a research team

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.615 line:0%
from Florida State University.

00:01:56.715 --> 00:02:00.519 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.856 line:0%
Very little is known about
these sumo-sized fish.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:06.626 line:0%
So, Chris is trying to
learn where they live,

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.795 line:0%
what they eat and whether
or not this protected giant

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:12.398 line:0%
is actually making a comeback.

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:16.202 line:0%
Not only is his research crucial
for their future survival,

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:19.405 line:0%
but it's the only way I
can legally examine one.

00:02:23.008 --> 00:02:24.977 line:0%
I don't see any
fishing rods on board,

00:02:25.077 --> 00:02:26.112 line:0%
how are we gonna
catch these fish?

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.380 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: The
easiest way that we've found

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.183 line:0%
to catch them is actually
through using a hand line.

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:31.317 line:0%
It's basically a rope.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.019 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: We're gonna be
catching grouper

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.119 line:0%
with a hand line.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.989 line:0%
This is big, heavy duty
gear, a thick rope,

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:40.326 line:0%
nine hundred pound
monofilament line.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.862 line:0%
And then, pretty much the
largest circle hook you can buy.

00:02:43.962 --> 00:02:46.231 line:0%
Look how big that hook is.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.068 line:0%
We wanna be able to
out-muscle the grouper,

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:52.204 line:0%
as soon as I feel a bite, I'm
gonna get into a tug-of-war

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:55.641 line:0%
with the grouper and I'm trying
to get it up to the surface.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:58.010 line:0%
Is there a chance we'll catch
fish other than grouper?

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.445 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: There's a lot
of different shark species

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:01.113 line:0%
in this area that we
end up pulling up.

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:02.315 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Some of
them pretty big?

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:03.683 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Some
big, I mean, we've caught

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.218 line:0%
14 foot hammerhead
during our last trip,

00:03:06.318 --> 00:03:07.487 line:0%
so these are massive fish.
ZEB HOGAN: Wow.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:13.359 line:0%
Sharks are a whole
different game.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:18.598 line:0%
When they get to the surface,
unlike goliath grouper,

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.567 line:0%
sharks will use their
jaw, their sharp teeth,

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.603 line:0%
they'll swing their
head and bite down!

00:03:27.339 --> 00:03:30.643 line:0%
So, if we catch one, we'll only
wanna bring it halfway

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.645 line:0%
into the boat to unhook it,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:36.149 line:0%
before releasing it
back into the ocean.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:37.450 line:0%
What bait are we gonna use?

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.285 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: So, we're
going to start off with a huge

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.587 line:0%
chunk of Bonita here.

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.625 line:0%
Okay. So, it's very important
when you're doing this

00:03:46.725 --> 00:03:48.094 line:0%
because we're catching
such large fish,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.328 line:0%
you've always gotta
watch your feet,

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:50.896 line:0%
'cause if a fish
or a shark grabs,

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:52.364 line:0%
it'll take you right
into the water.

00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:54.066 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Okay.

00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:55.868 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: So,
just chuck it over.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.470 line:0%
All right. Then hold on to
that mono,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.938 line:0%
keeping that in front of you,
good.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:06.045 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: If that float starts
bobbing or goes underneath

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:09.248 line:0%
the water, that's an indication
that I have a big fish.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:13.019 line:0%
But I have no way of knowing
if I've hooked a shark

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.154 line:0%
or a goliath grouper.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.189 line:0%
I just know that it's
gonna be a big fight.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:26.832 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:29.535 line:0%
Wow, there we go, got one!

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:30.336 line:0%
Fish!

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:38.644 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:41.114 line:0%
It's coming towards the
boat, hand over hand.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:46.118 line:0%
If it's a grouper, it's
gonna be a monster.

00:04:46.218 --> 00:04:47.687 line:0%
Oh, oh, here we go.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.054 line:0%
Shaking, shaking.

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:51.590 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.926 line:0%
It's coming up, can
feel it tug,

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:57.597 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:58.798 line:0%
shaking.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:04.037 line:0%
Oh, I see white, it's
a shark, it's spinning.

00:05:06.438 --> 00:05:07.807 line:0%
Oh.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.214 line:0%
Woo! Man!

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:18.050 line:0%
Sandbar shark, see the huge
dorsal fin on the back,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.619 line:0%
that's characteristic
of a sandbar shark.

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:21.921 line:0%
Making another run!

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.330 line:0%
We're gonna try to get this
into the boat head-first.

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:35.100 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Okay, hold,
that's enough, that's enough,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.001 line:0%
that's enough.
JESS: I got it. I got it.

00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:38.705 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Yeah? There you go.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:42.507 line:0%
Yeah. It's a big, probably
seven, eight feet long,

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.944 line:0%
you can see the big
dorsal fin, big pec fins.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.179 line:0%
Watch out!

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:49.948 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Got slapped
in the face that was fun!

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.517 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: And these
sharks, they can twist,

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:54.587 line:0%
they'll throw up their
head, you can see,

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.689 line:0%
if it senses something close
to it, it'll bite down.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:58.524 line:0%
So, right there,
it's kind of blocked,

00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:01.927 line:0%
it can't really move its head
too much and twist around

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:05.230 line:0%
for a bite, so this is the
safest place to keep the shark.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:05.931 line:0%
Got a tag?

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:07.233 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Got one.

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:09.534 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: We're
gonna clip the hook,

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.704 line:0%
so it doesn't have its
hook in its jaw anymore.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:15.908 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Let's go.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.043 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Okay,
it's ready to go.

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:18.644 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Ready?

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:27.953 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Nice.

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:28.988 line:0%
Good work, guys.

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.456 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Yeah.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.590 line:0%
That was good.

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.061 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: That really
was a monster of the deep,

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:39.732 line:0%
but I wanna know if the goliath
grouper's bite is just as big.

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:42.735 line:0%
So, it's time to
bait up and cast out.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:51.543 line:0%
Ugh, fish!

00:06:51.643 --> 00:06:52.611 line:0%
Oh!

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:54.814 line:0%
Gah, it feels like we
hooked onto an anchor!

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.448 line:0%
Go round the other side?

00:06:56.548 --> 00:06:57.550 line:0%
Ugh!

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:01.921 line:0%
It's running. It's running.

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:03.789 line:0%
Watch it, watch it,
watch it, watch it.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:07.827 line:0%
Let it go, let it go, let it go.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.962 line:0%
Waaaa.

00:07:11.563 --> 00:07:15.500 line:0%
It's a large shark, big
Dusky, it's hard to maneuver,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.637 line:0%
it's probably eight
or nine feet long.

00:07:19.371 --> 00:07:20.406 line:0%
JESS: Watch it, watch it.

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:23.209 line:0%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.379 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Here it is.
You got the tail rope?

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:27.679 line:0%
JESS: Yeah, nice.

00:07:27.779 --> 00:07:30.282 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: We got
it, let him go!

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:32.852 line:0%
Hey, we got the tail, so now,
we can control it a little bit

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:35.521 line:0%
easier, and we're gonna bring
it in tail-first into the boat.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.490 line:0%
It's a really beautiful shark.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:39.859 line:0%
Watch out.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:44.263 line:0%
Got it?

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:46.565 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Here
we go, here we go!

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:48.301 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Oh, my God.

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:54.073 line:0%
We got to cut the hook out.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:58.810 line:0%
Woo, man!

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:00.746 line:0%
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:01.614 line:0%
That was a big fish!

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:09.054 line:0%
Sharks are clearly an
apex predator out here.

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:11.123 line:0%
They're so quick to our bait.

00:08:11.223 --> 00:08:14.093 line:0%
We're gonna need to drop more
hooks to increase our chances

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:16.095 line:0%
of catching a goliath grouper.

00:08:18.597 --> 00:08:19.665 line:0%
Oh.

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:21.333 line:0%
Fish on.

00:08:21.433 --> 00:08:22.902 line:0%
Oh, man!

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:23.936 line:0%
Fish.

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:25.004 line:0%
It's really heavy.

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:26.739 line:0%
It's hard to hold on to it.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.642 line:0%
That really is like
tug of war, isn't it?

00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:32.277 line:0%
Feel free to help me.

00:08:32.377 --> 00:08:34.947 line:0%
Three people, three of us.

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:37.850 line:0%
Goliath grouper can weigh
as much as a grizzly bear.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.786 line:0%
And right now, that's
what this fish feels like.

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:42.054 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Pull,
pull, pull, pull, pull.

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.122 line:0%
Think we got it!

00:08:44.223 --> 00:08:45.291 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: We're
up to the float.

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:46.225 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Keep pulling.
Just keep pulling.

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:46.859 line:0%
Keep pulling.
ZEB HOGAN: Keep pulling.

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:48.093 line:0%
Just watching my feet.

00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:48.760 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Keep going,
keep going, keep going.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.696 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Aghh!

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:51.630 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Remember
to watch your hands

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.566 line:0%
on the leader part.
ZEB HOGAN: Yep!

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:56.668 line:0%
Oh, man, it feels heavy.

00:08:56.768 --> 00:08:58.737 line:0%
Goliath grouper, big one!

00:08:58.837 --> 00:09:00.038 line:0%
Yeah, it looks big.

00:09:00.138 --> 00:09:03.242 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:04.877 line:0%
Ready to get it on board?

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:06.912 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Here it comes.

00:09:07.012 --> 00:09:09.314 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Nice, awesome, man!

00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:10.549 line:0%
Big fish.

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:14.954 line:0%
So, first, goliath grouper,
big, man. It's massive!

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:17.390 line:0%
Towel over the head of
the fish to keep it calm.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.992 line:0%
A tube in the fish's mouth to
pass water over its gills.

00:09:21.092 --> 00:09:23.895 line:0%
And we're gonna strap it down,
so in case it starts flapping,

00:09:23.995 --> 00:09:27.099 line:0%
it stays secured
to this stretcher.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:30.870 line:0%
Check it out, our first
grouper, man, it's a big fish.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.239 line:0%
They're one of the largest
bony fish on earth.

00:09:34.339 --> 00:09:38.043 line:0%
Grow up to about eight feet
long, over six hundred pounds.

00:09:38.143 --> 00:09:40.445 line:0%
Everything about this
fish is super-sized.

00:09:40.546 --> 00:09:44.349 line:0%
You can see big head,
large mouth, thick lips.

00:09:44.449 --> 00:09:48.053 line:0%
The dorsal spines are almost
the size of one of my fingers.

00:09:48.154 --> 00:09:51.223 line:0%
And this kind of classic
grouper mottled color.

00:09:51.323 --> 00:09:54.626 line:0%
Lighter belly, darker back.

00:09:54.726 --> 00:09:56.595 line:0%
One of the most impressive
things about the goliath

00:09:56.695 --> 00:09:59.565 line:0%
grouper, not necessarily
the length, but its mass,

00:09:59.665 --> 00:10:01.967 line:0%
the thickness, all of the
muscle, the strength.

00:10:02.067 --> 00:10:05.003 line:0%
And that's what I'm feeling
when I'm holding on to that

00:10:05.103 --> 00:10:07.672 line:0%
hand line, is this
fish using its strength

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:10.309 line:0%
to try to get back down
to the ocean bottom.

00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:11.276 line:0%
JESS: When you're
ready, pull tight,

00:10:11.376 --> 00:10:12.711 line:0%
and let me know
what number you got.

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:13.478 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Okay.

00:10:13.578 --> 00:10:14.980 line:0%
One five three, is that right?

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:16.549 line:0%
JESS: Yep, one five three.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:17.816 line:0%
Awesome!

00:10:17.916 --> 00:10:19.184 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: This is a
five-foot Goliath grouper.

00:10:19.284 --> 00:10:20.619 line:0%
How much would you
guess this fish weighs?

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:21.420 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Probably
around 200 pounds.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:22.454 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: That's a big fish.

00:10:22.554 --> 00:10:23.922 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Yeah,
that's a big fish.

00:10:24.022 --> 00:10:26.926 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Amazingly, this
grouper could get four times

00:10:27.026 --> 00:10:30.329 line:0%
heavier and weigh eight
hundred pounds by the time

00:10:30.429 --> 00:10:33.232 line:0%
it's fully grown.

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:36.468 line:0%
Chris wants to find out what
the goliath grouper is eating.

00:10:36.568 --> 00:10:39.204 line:0%
And he's figured out a bizarre
but effective way to do

00:10:39.304 --> 00:10:42.808 line:0%
just that with a plastic pipe.

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.643 line:0%
The easiest way to get the
stomach contents is actually

00:10:44.743 --> 00:10:48.280 line:0%
put my hand and my arm all the
way into the fish's belly.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:49.581 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Go all
the way in until you feel

00:10:49.681 --> 00:10:52.018 line:0%
the back of its stomach.
ZEB HOGAN: Ugh!

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:54.586 line:0%
All the way?

00:10:54.686 --> 00:10:56.288 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI:
All the way in.

00:10:56.388 --> 00:10:58.624 line:0%
Feel around for anything
that might be in there.

00:10:58.724 --> 00:11:01.293 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Oh, gross!

00:11:01.393 --> 00:11:02.961 line:0%
It doesn't smell
very good either!

00:11:03.062 --> 00:11:04.896 line:0%
Feels like I'm putting
my hand into a roast,

00:11:04.996 --> 00:11:07.299 line:0%
or like a raw steak.

00:11:07.399 --> 00:11:09.701 line:0%
I can't tell what
stomach and what's bait.

00:11:09.801 --> 00:11:11.604 line:0%
Oh, there's something in here.
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Yeah. Yep.

00:11:11.704 --> 00:11:13.939 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Agh!

00:11:14.039 --> 00:11:15.707 line:0%
[LAUGHS]
That's gross.

00:11:15.807 --> 00:11:18.076 line:0%
Agh!

00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:19.645 line:0%
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:19.745 --> 00:11:21.546 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: It's
partially digested fish parts.

00:11:21.646 --> 00:11:22.981 line:0%
JESS: We'll take it.
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: All right.

00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:24.950 line:0%
So, put that in here
as our stomach samples.

00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:29.387 line:0%
So, you can see here, that's
a crab part, right there,

00:11:29.487 --> 00:11:32.057 line:0%
you can also see
fairly small vertebrae,

00:11:32.157 --> 00:11:33.959 line:0%
which will be from
a smaller reef fish,

00:11:34.059 --> 00:11:35.560 line:0%
probably not
something very large.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.096 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: I was expecting to
find large fish in this stomach.

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:40.666 line:0%
The groupers' normal
diet it's not large fish?

00:11:40.766 --> 00:11:42.568 line:0%
It's crabs and smaller fish?

00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:44.002 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Exactly,
behaviorally, they don't,

00:11:44.102 --> 00:11:45.671 line:0%
they don't swim very fast, so
they're not going to be after

00:11:45.771 --> 00:11:48.139 line:0%
very fast prey, they often
feed into the bottom,

00:11:48.239 --> 00:11:49.742 line:0%
into the sediment.
ZEB HOGAN: Wow. So,

00:11:49.842 --> 00:11:52.378 line:0%
even though the goliath
grouper is able to swallow

00:11:52.478 --> 00:11:56.281 line:0%
a big bait, normally,
grouper feed relatively low

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:59.452 line:0%
on the food chain, on
crabs and small fish.

00:12:01.153 --> 00:12:03.255 line:0%
And that's an important clue.

00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:06.258 line:0%
This goliath grouper wasn't
searching the ocean looking

00:12:06.358 --> 00:12:08.960 line:0%
for big prey like
those sharks were.

00:12:09.061 --> 00:12:11.130 line:0%
To hook this grouper,
I had to drop my bait

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.332 line:0%
right in front of it on
the ocean bottom.

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:14.099 line:0%
Okay.

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:14.933 line:0%
JESS: Hold.
ZEB HOGAN: Okay.

00:12:15.033 --> 00:12:16.635 line:0%
You're ready?
JESS: Clamp it!

00:12:16.735 --> 00:12:17.570 line:0%
Perfect.
ZEB HOGAN: Hey.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.438 line:0%
Okay. Fish is tagged.

00:12:19.538 --> 00:12:20.772 line:0%
We ready to get this
fish back into water?

00:12:20.872 --> 00:12:22.842 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: We're ready to
get it back in the water then.

00:12:24.643 --> 00:12:26.712 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: But before I
can release this grouper,

00:12:26.812 --> 00:12:29.381 line:0%
something big is
hitting my other bait.

00:12:29.481 --> 00:12:31.517 line:0%
Aggghhhhh! Fish on.

00:12:31.617 --> 00:12:33.285 line:0%
Oh, my God, it's huge!

00:12:36.888 --> 00:12:37.957 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: We got a fish!

00:12:40.893 --> 00:12:43.161 line:0%
It's just like pulling a
sack of cement, isn't it?

00:12:43.261 --> 00:12:45.297 line:0%
Agh, looks like a grouper.

00:12:45.397 --> 00:12:47.733 line:0%
Seeing that pale,
kinda yellow color.

00:12:47.833 --> 00:12:48.934 line:0%
Arghh.

00:12:49.034 --> 00:12:52.171 line:0%
I'm fighting the fish and
the current trying to get it

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:53.438 line:0%
up behind the boat here.

00:12:53.538 --> 00:12:54.740 line:0%
Let's get a look at it.

00:12:58.376 --> 00:13:00.345 line:0%
Woo, oh, man.

00:13:00.445 --> 00:13:01.213 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: I'd have
to say that's gonna be

00:13:01.313 --> 00:13:02.481 line:0%
five and half feet long.

00:13:02.581 --> 00:13:04.517 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: So, we caught
two fish at the same time.

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:06.551 line:0%
These guys say
that isn't unusual,

00:13:06.651 --> 00:13:11.023 line:0%
once we get the first bite, then
other grouper can sense

00:13:11.123 --> 00:13:13.358 line:0%
what's going on and they'll
come in and feed as well,

00:13:13.458 --> 00:13:16.262 line:0%
creating a feeding frenzy.

00:13:16.362 --> 00:13:18.564 line:0%
So, check out this mouth and
what you can see immediately

00:13:18.664 --> 00:13:22.767 line:0%
it's not like a shark
with sharp, jagged teeth.

00:13:22.867 --> 00:13:27.739 line:0%
On the upper and lower jaw are
pads of needle-like teeth

00:13:27.839 --> 00:13:31.644 line:0%
that are pointed backwards, so
if I put my finger in its mouth,

00:13:31.744 --> 00:13:33.412 line:0%
and try to pull my
finger out, it grips,

00:13:33.512 --> 00:13:36.315 line:0%
so that's exactly what would
happen to prey that ended up

00:13:36.415 --> 00:13:38.217 line:0%
in a grouper's mouth.

00:13:38.317 --> 00:13:41.053 line:0%
They can open their mouth
very, very, very wide,

00:13:41.153 --> 00:13:43.689 line:0%
the grouper will suck
in prey, clamp down,

00:13:43.789 --> 00:13:46.992 line:0%
and use those teeth to grip
on to prey and then swallow

00:13:47.092 --> 00:13:48.894 line:0%
the prey whole.

00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:50.228 line:0%
So, this is an important clue.

00:13:50.328 --> 00:13:54.232 line:0%
Groupers are not biting and
ripping apart prey like a shark,

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:56.969 line:0%
they're sucking it in
and swallowing it whole.

00:13:57.069 --> 00:14:00.472 line:0%
Which makes total sense now
that I know grouper eat mainly

00:14:00.572 --> 00:14:05.477 line:0%
small fish and crabs, hardly
the diet of a ferocious monster.

00:14:05.577 --> 00:14:08.180 line:0%
It's a re-capture, so this
is a fish that these guys

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.215 line:0%
have already caught
and tagged once.

00:14:10.315 --> 00:14:15.020 line:0%
Okay, tag number 96F2508.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.288 line:0%
JESS: Got it.

00:14:16.388 --> 00:14:18.223 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: What are the tags and
re-captures tell you about

00:14:18.323 --> 00:14:19.624 line:0%
the fish's movement patterns?

00:14:19.724 --> 00:14:20.993 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: If we're
recapturing this fish

00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:22.994 line:0%
over and over again, that means
that they're likely residence

00:14:23.095 --> 00:14:24.897 line:0%
because they have a very
strong site fidelity,

00:14:24.997 --> 00:14:27.966 line:0%
they don't move a lot during
most time of the year.

00:14:28.066 --> 00:14:30.302 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: So, that's
an important clue.

00:14:30.402 --> 00:14:32.104 line:0%
Goliath grouper
aren't like sharks,

00:14:32.204 --> 00:14:35.941 line:0%
patrolling large expanses
of ocean looking for prey.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.477 line:0%
They'll find a wreck
or a reef they like

00:14:38.577 --> 00:14:40.379 line:0%
and make that their home.

00:14:41.379 --> 00:14:42.647 line:0%
Unstrap it first.
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Yes.

00:14:42.747 --> 00:14:45.250 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Strap's off.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:45.951 line:0%
CHRIS MALINOWSKI: Good.

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:46.585 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: See you later.

00:14:47.819 --> 00:14:49.121 line:0%
Wow!

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:56.862 line:0%
Working with Chris and his
research team and catching

00:14:56.962 --> 00:14:58.864 line:0%
two big ocean predators,

00:14:58.964 --> 00:15:02.334 line:0%
I now know that goliath
grouper are misunderstood.

00:15:02.434 --> 00:15:05.337 line:0%
Even though goliath grouper
are big, powerful predators,

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:07.806 line:0%
they're not feeding on
the top of the food chain.

00:15:07.906 --> 00:15:10.675 line:0%
They're not violently ripping
up prey with their teeth

00:15:10.775 --> 00:15:14.613 line:0%
like some sharks, they're
sucking in prey with their mouth

00:15:14.713 --> 00:15:17.916 line:0%
And goliath grouper aren't
out patrolling the open ocean

00:15:18.016 --> 00:15:19.718 line:0%
like those sharks I caught.

00:15:19.818 --> 00:15:22.353 line:0%
They're sticking to one
chosen reef or wreck,

00:15:22.453 --> 00:15:25.090 line:0%
finding small prey
on the ocean bottom.

00:15:27.525 --> 00:15:30.662 line:0%
So, why are there viral
videos showing them attacking

00:15:30.762 --> 00:15:32.530 line:0%
large prey on the surface?

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.300 line:0%
MAN: Oh, oh, my God!

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.335 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:15:37.435 --> 00:15:39.738 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: I leave Chris and
travel across state to the Gulf

00:15:39.838 --> 00:15:43.275 line:0%
of Mexico, where some of these
viral videos have emerged.

00:15:44.943 --> 00:15:48.747 line:0%
It's here in the small town of
Englewood where local fishermen

00:15:48.847 --> 00:15:51.149 line:0%
are gonna help me set
the record straight.

00:15:51.883 --> 00:15:54.119 line:0%
I wanna see whether
or not the viral videos

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:57.256 line:0%
of goliath grouper are true.

00:15:57.356 --> 00:16:00.859 line:0%
And if so, figure out why
a bottom-dwelling fish

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.895 line:0%
is behaving so aggressively
at the surface.

00:16:03.995 --> 00:16:08.400 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:09.501 line:0%
I'm out with Dan.

00:16:09.601 --> 00:16:11.737 line:0%
He's a Florida local,
a charter boat captain,

00:16:11.837 --> 00:16:14.339 line:0%
and he has encounters with
goliath grouper every day

00:16:14.439 --> 00:16:17.076 line:0%
and this is something I'd
like to see for myself.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:20.778 line:0%
We're about five, six
miles offshore here,

00:16:20.879 --> 00:16:23.882 line:0%
fishing above an
artificial reef.

00:16:23.982 --> 00:16:27.219 line:0%
This is a popular fishing reef
where Dan says goliath grouper

00:16:27.319 --> 00:16:31.357 line:0%
come to the surface
to feed aggressively.

00:16:31.457 --> 00:16:32.757 line:0%
Oh, got a nibble.

00:16:32.857 --> 00:16:34.259 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:16:34.359 --> 00:16:35.727 line:0%
Got something.

00:16:35.827 --> 00:16:36.461 line:0%
Fish on!

00:16:36.561 --> 00:16:43.268 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:16:43.368 --> 00:16:44.269 line:0%
Oh!

00:16:44.369 --> 00:16:45.336 line:0%
Lost the fish.

00:16:45.436 --> 00:16:46.838 line:0%
Something big came
in and got it.

00:16:46.939 --> 00:16:48.240 line:0%
DAN BOURCIER: Yeah, you
probably had about a

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.675 line:0%
three, four-pound fish on there
and then a goliath grouper came

00:16:50.775 --> 00:16:51.977 line:0%
and got him about halfway up.

00:16:52.077 --> 00:16:53.545 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: So, that's what
people are complaining about.

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:56.348 line:0%
People come out here to
try to catch smaller fish,

00:16:56.448 --> 00:16:59.384 line:0%
the goliath grouper
come up, whack.

00:16:59.484 --> 00:17:03.922 line:0%
So, I witnessed firsthand a
grouper steal a fish off my line

00:17:04.022 --> 00:17:07.292 line:0%
and I know there is some truth
to those stories I heard.

00:17:07.392 --> 00:17:08.693 line:0%
So, how do you avoid that Dan?

00:17:08.793 --> 00:17:10.962 line:0%
DAN BOURCIER: Well, the best
trick is when you feel that bite

00:17:11.062 --> 00:17:13.832 line:0%
you just reel as quick as you
can to try and get that fish

00:17:13.932 --> 00:17:15.100 line:0%
away from the bottom.

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:16.301 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: And so, as soon
as the fish is hooked,

00:17:16.401 --> 00:17:18.403 line:0%
it's a race because there's
probably a big grouper

00:17:18.503 --> 00:17:19.470 line:0%
chasing it?

00:17:19.571 --> 00:17:19.872 line:0%
DAN BOURCIER: You hit
it right on the head.

00:17:22.107 --> 00:17:22.941 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Fish, fish!
DAN BOURCIER: There you go!

00:17:23.041 --> 00:17:25.210 line:0%
Get him up, get
him up, get him up!

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.345 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: Just trying to
get him up as fast as I can.

00:17:27.445 --> 00:17:30.215 line:0%
So, it's a race now
between me and the grouper.

00:17:30.315 --> 00:17:31.683 line:0%
Agh!

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:36.288 line:0%
God.

00:17:36.388 --> 00:17:37.389 line:0%
DAN BOURCIER: Come on!
Go! Go! Go!

00:17:37.489 --> 00:17:39.124 line:0%
Get him off the bottom. Come on!
ZEB HOGAN: Agh!

00:17:42.393 --> 00:17:44.128 line:0%
Woo!

00:17:44.228 --> 00:17:45.363 line:0%
Woo!

00:17:45.463 --> 00:17:47.632 line:0%
As long as I reel in fast, get
the fish up to the boat

00:17:47.732 --> 00:17:49.968 line:0%
as quickly as possible,
no problems.

00:17:50.068 --> 00:17:51.503 line:0%
DAN BOURCIER: That extra
two seconds that that fish

00:17:51.603 --> 00:17:55.373 line:0%
either has to rest or to swim
back down, that's all it takes.

00:17:55.473 --> 00:17:57.776 line:0%
Your goliaths are gonna
get 'em most of the time.

00:17:57.876 --> 00:18:01.480 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: The viral videos make
a lot more sense to me now.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.948 line:0%
People are going out to
locations that have a lot of

00:18:04.048 --> 00:18:05.250 line:0%
goliath grouper.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.085 line:0%
They're putting bait in
the water or they have

00:18:07.185 --> 00:18:09.554 line:0%
a weakened fish on the line
and the goliath grouper

00:18:09.655 --> 00:18:12.524 line:0%
are coming in for an easy meal.

00:18:12.624 --> 00:18:15.994 line:0%
MAN: Oh, oh, oh, my God!

00:18:16.094 --> 00:18:17.161 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:18:17.261 --> 00:18:18.496 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: So, even
though they catch,

00:18:18.596 --> 00:18:22.433 line:0%
the majority of their diet on
the ocean bottom, it's clear,

00:18:22.534 --> 00:18:25.404 line:0%
goliath grouper will
still target a big meal

00:18:25.504 --> 00:18:28.439 line:0%
if it's dangled in front of
them or it's on the surface.

00:18:28.539 --> 00:18:32.076 line:0%
I wanna see if this 'easy meal'
theory explains the attacks

00:18:32.176 --> 00:18:34.078 line:0%
on scuba divers.

00:18:34.179 --> 00:18:37.048 line:0%
To do that, I'm going
out with Tom McLaughlin.

00:18:37.148 --> 00:18:40.952 line:0%
He's been fishing and diving for
over 20 years and has a secret

00:18:41.053 --> 00:18:44.222 line:0%
spot where I can dive
with goliath grouper.

00:18:44.322 --> 00:18:45.524 line:0%
So, where are we
headed right now?

00:18:45.624 --> 00:18:46.758 line:0%
TOM MCLAUGHLIN: We're headed
to a big wreck in about

00:18:46.858 --> 00:18:47.926 line:0%
a hundred feet of water.

00:18:48.026 --> 00:18:50.028 line:0%
It's the kind of place
they really like to live.

00:18:50.128 --> 00:18:52.364 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: The entire wreck
is about 500-feet long,

00:18:52.464 --> 00:18:53.598 line:0%
it's all broken apart.

00:18:53.698 --> 00:18:56.835 line:0%
We're gonna focus in on
one piece of that wreck

00:18:56.935 --> 00:18:59.771 line:0%
where Tom says the grouper
really like to hang out.

00:18:59.871 --> 00:19:08.647 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:19:08.747 --> 00:19:10.449 line:0%
This is my first dive.

00:19:10.549 --> 00:19:12.584 line:0%
We're in about a hundred feet
of water diving on a wreck

00:19:12.684 --> 00:19:14.619 line:0%
where Tom's seen a
lot of groupers.

00:19:14.719 --> 00:19:16.421 line:0%
He's also seen a lot of
big sharks around here,

00:19:16.521 --> 00:19:18.823 line:0%
so I'm excited, feeling
a little bit nervous,

00:19:18.923 --> 00:19:20.892 line:0%
just wanna get in the
water and get down and see

00:19:20.992 --> 00:19:22.494 line:0%
what's down there.

00:19:34.473 --> 00:19:38.143 line:0%
I love the water, but that
sense of not being able to see

00:19:38.243 --> 00:19:42.181 line:0%
what's out there is still a
little bit unnerving.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:50.722 line:0%
Out of the murk, the gloom I
see the large shape of a wreck

00:19:50.822 --> 00:19:52.457 line:0%
emerge below me.

00:19:56.427 --> 00:19:57.929 line:0%
And I need to be careful.

00:19:58.029 --> 00:20:01.499 line:0%
Swimming in confined spaces
like this can be dangerous.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.503 line:0%
There are lots of things
I can get tangled up in.

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:09.741 line:0%
Suddenly, I look up and in
front of me is a big shadow,

00:20:09.841 --> 00:20:10.876 line:0%
moving in the darkness.

00:20:14.412 --> 00:20:17.315 line:0%
And as my eyes adjust
to the darkness,

00:20:17.415 --> 00:20:20.852 line:0%
I'm face to face
with the grouper.

00:20:20.952 --> 00:20:23.322 line:0%
And I'm not sure how
it's going to react.

00:20:25.757 --> 00:20:30.195 line:0%
It seems to sense my presence
and it slowly moves away.

00:20:31.462 --> 00:20:34.232 line:0%
I'm seeing grouper poking their
heads out of the portholes

00:20:34.332 --> 00:20:35.266 line:0%
in the wreck.

00:20:35.366 --> 00:20:37.302 line:0%
Swimming up a ledge,
peering over a crack,

00:20:37.402 --> 00:20:40.539 line:0%
and seeing a grouper sitting
down in the hole of the wreck.

00:20:46.711 --> 00:20:48.480 line:0%
None of the grouper
are being aggressive.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.049 line:0%
So, now, I wanna try the
second half of my experiment

00:20:53.018 --> 00:20:54.953 line:0%
and introduce an easy meal.

00:20:56.954 --> 00:21:00.258 line:0%
We're sitting on top of the
wreck and a big school of jacks

00:21:00.358 --> 00:21:01.827 line:0%
swims by us.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:11.036 line:0%
The second Tom spears that jack,

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:13.772 line:0%
the atmosphere
on the wreck changes.

00:21:14.673 --> 00:21:18.810 line:0%
The reef becomes more tense,
more turned on, more energized.

00:21:20.845 --> 00:21:23.648 line:0%
As we're swimming up the wreck,
I see off to my right something

00:21:23.749 --> 00:21:25.684 line:0%
big coming out of the darkness.

00:21:26.684 --> 00:21:28.019 line:0%
It's not a grouper.

00:21:28.119 --> 00:21:30.054 line:0%
It's a bull shark.

00:21:30.155 --> 00:21:32.257 line:0%
One of the most dangerous
sharks in the world.

00:21:33.224 --> 00:21:34.993 line:0%
And it's coming right at us.

00:21:39.497 --> 00:21:45.003 line:0%
♪ [MUSIC] ♪

00:21:45.103 --> 00:21:47.438 line:0%
ZEB HOGAN: I see this light
shape coming towards me,

00:21:47.538 --> 00:21:51.610 line:0%
pointed snout, sleek body, more
streamlined than a grouper.

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:55.647 line:0%
A seven-foot bull shark, it
comes in between Tom and I,

00:21:55.747 --> 00:21:57.749 line:0%
and circles back behind my head.

00:22:01.019 --> 00:22:03.421 line:0%
This isn't a good situation.

00:22:03.521 --> 00:22:06.524 line:0%
A lot of people get bitten
by sharks in exactly

00:22:06.624 --> 00:22:08.359 line:0%
these circumstances.

00:22:08.459 --> 00:22:10.528 line:0%
And it's circling us,
swimming around us.

00:22:10.629 --> 00:22:13.665 line:0%
It's obviously cued in
on that bait as well.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:18.470 line:0%
We need to get out
of this situation.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:22.674 line:0%
We move down to a crack in
the wreck where we'll be less

00:22:22.774 --> 00:22:24.943 line:0%
vulnerable to the bull shark.

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:28.847 line:0%
But the crack in the wreck
is where all the grouper

00:22:28.947 --> 00:22:29.648 line:0%
are sitting.

00:22:30.982 --> 00:22:33.584 line:0%
And the grouper are also
behaving differently

00:22:33.684 --> 00:22:36.688 line:0%
because we have this fish.

00:22:36.788 --> 00:22:40.792 line:0%
The grouper are being much more
aggressive, moving more quickly,

00:22:40.892 --> 00:22:44.296 line:0%
obviously interested in
this fish that we have.

00:22:47.732 --> 00:22:51.536 line:0%
I pick up the fish, hold it
out and an instant a grouper

00:22:51.636 --> 00:22:55.940 line:0%
comes in, grabs the whole head
of the fish, of this jack

00:22:56.040 --> 00:23:00.412 line:0%
